Light dot: oldest chart found. Dark dot: today’s bike. Brown dot: a generation in between. Bikes are sorted by how much they changed. For head angle, a move left means slacker steering.

{
  const ch = lineages.map(l =&gt; {
    const g = overTime.filter(d =&gt; d.lineage === l).sort((a, b) =&gt; a.chart_year - b.chart_year);
    const f = g[0], c = g.at(-1);
    return {l, hta: c.head_tube_angle - f.head_tube_angle, wb: c.wheelbase - f.wheelbase, reach: c.reach - f.reach, drop: c.bb_drop - f.bb_drop, cs: c.chainstay - f.chainstay};
  });
  const n = ch.length;
  const count = (k, test) =&gt; ch.filter(d =&gt; test(d[k])).length;
  const med = k =&gt; median(ch.map(d =&gt; d[k]));
  const norco = ch.find(d =&gt; d.l === "Norco Search");
  return md`**What it shows.** Gravel bikes have got slacker, lower, and longer at the back, but not simply longer in reach.

- **Slacker steering.** ${count("hta", v =&gt; v &lt; -0.25)} of ${n} bikes now have a slacker head angle than their oldest chart (median change ${med("hta") &lt; 0 ? "−" : "+"}${Math.abs(med("hta")).toFixed(1)}°). The Norco changed most, by ${Math.abs(norco.hta).toFixed(2)}°.
- **Lower bottom brackets.** ${count("drop", v =&gt; v &gt; 2)} of ${n} have more BB drop, which sits the rider lower between the wheels, and ${count("drop", v =&gt; v &lt; -2) === 0 ? "none has less" : `${count("drop", v =&gt; v &lt; -2)} have less`}.
- **Longer at the back.** ${count("cs", v =&gt; v &gt; 2)} of ${n} have longer chainstays (more tyre room), and ${count("cs", v =&gt; v &lt; -2) === 0 ? "none is shorter" : `${count("cs", v =&gt; v &lt; -2)} are shorter`}.
- **Wheelbase mostly grew.** ${count("wb", v =&gt; v &gt; 5)} of ${n} are more than 5 mm longer between the axles (median ${med("wb") &gt; 0 ? "+" : ""}${med("wb").toFixed(0)} mm). The Norco grew most, by ${norco.wb.toFixed(0)} mm.
- **Reach went both ways.** ${count("reach", v =&gt; v &gt; 5)} bikes got more than 5 mm longer and ${count("reach", v =&gt; v &lt; -5)} got more than 5 mm shorter, for the same handlebar height. Where reach shrank, the newer bike puts the rider more upright rather than more stretched out.

Unlike mountain bikes, there's no single "longer" trend. The consistent change is lower and slacker, with longer chainstays for wider tyres.`;
}






Sources: archived copies of each manufacturer’s product page, listed with capture dates in data/gravel_geometry_history.csv and on the Data and methods page. Limits: these are 7 bikes that still exist today, not a sample of the market. The first Canyon Grail used a two-tier handlebar on most builds, so its frame stack understates where the hands sat and its “same rider” size reads long. The 2021 Specialized Crux isn’t included because its archived page didn’t save the geometry.
